CASE Statement in SQL

The CASE statement is used to apply conditional logic in SQL, similar to if–else statements in programming.

It allows you to create new values based on conditions.

# Basic Syntax
SELECT 
    column_name,
    CASE 
        WHEN condition1 THEN result1
        WHEN condition2 THEN result2
        ELSE result3
    END AS new_column
FROM table_name;

Explanation:

🔹WHEN → condition to check

🔹THEN → value returned if condition is true

🔹ELSE → value returned if no conditions match

🔹END → closes the CASE statement

# Types of CASE

1️⃣ Simple CASE

SELECT 
    cust_id, 
    CASE city
        WHEN 'mumbai' THEN 'Zone 1'
        WHEN 'Chennai' THEN 'Zone 2'
        ELSE 'Other Zone'
    END AS ZONE
FROM cust_dimen;

2️⃣ Searched CASE (More Common)

SELECT 
    sales,
    CASE
        WHEN sales > 1000 THEN 'High'
        ELSE 'Low'
    END
FROM market_fact_full;

# Key Points

🔹Used in SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY, GROUP BY

🔹Helps in data classification

🔹Very common in reporting and dashboards

EXAMPLE:

USE market_star_schema;

-- classify sales as "Loss", "Profit", or "Neither Profit nor Loss"

SELECT profit, 
CASE 
	WHEN profit<0 THEN "Loss"
    WHEN profit>0 THEN "Profit"
    ELSE "Neither Profit nor Loss"
END AS profit_description
FROM market_fact_full
HAVING profit_description="Loss";

/* discount: (0 to 0.01: Very Less discount), (0.02 to 0.05: Less discount), 
(0.06 to 0.1: Good Discount), (>0.1: Heavy Discount), No discount */

SELECT discount, 
CASE
	WHEN discount>0 AND discount<=0.01 THEN "Very Less discount"
    WHEN discount BETWEEN 0.02 AND 0.05 THEN "Less discount"
    WHEN discount BETWEEN 0.06 AND 0.1 THEN "Good discount"
    WHEN discount>0.1 THEN "Heavy discount"
    ELSE "No discount"
END AS discount_description
FROM market_fact_full;

/* calculate avg_profit for each product sub category and create a column called as 
avg_profit_desciption avg_profit: (<=0: Loss, (1-100): Less profit, (101-500): Good Profit
(>500: Heavy Profit)). Get the rows with avg_profit_description as Loss. */

SELECT product_sub_category, AVG(profit) AS avg_profit,
CASE
	WHEN AVG(profit)<=0 THEN "Loss"
    WHEN AVG(profit) BETWEEN 1 AND 100 THEN "Less Profit"
    WHEN AVG(profit) BETWEEN 101 AND 500 THEN "Good Profit"
    ELSE "Heavy Profit"
END AS avg_profit_desciption
FROM market_fact_full mff
JOIN prod_dimen pd ON mff.prod_id=pd.prod_id
GROUP BY product_sub_category
HAVING avg_profit_desciption="Loss";